From yurii.aulchenko at gmail.com  Tue Oct 18 18:36:42 2011
From: yurii.aulchenko at gmail.com (Yury Aulchenko)
Date: Tue, 18 Oct 2011 23:36:42 +0700
Subject: [GenABEL-dev] a couple of questions concerning ERC grant
Message-ID: <35F2F7C9-836A-492A-8104-42088E9D2A37@gmail.com>

Dear All,

I am going to apply for an ERC starting grant this year, and this application will assume the GenABEL project (not just the suite!) as a framework for methodology development. 

I think of asking money for the development of new methods (so all of us people excited about genomics can have more opportunities within the suite); GenABEL core development/redesign, so all GenABEL suite packages can benefit from it; some 'community' money for optimization 'sub-grants' (e.g. if you have a great idea and a prototype, but some professional programming needs to be done before the software is really fast, optimal and friendly, you could apply for some professional programmers time); also some money for community development (ideas: partly sponsored courses, workshops, visitor programs for these who want to develop methodology). 

Few questions to you: 

 -- Would you support this idea in general (in which case I will greatly appreciate a letter from you to support the grant application, please drop me an e-mail)

-- Is that OK with you if I announce that I am applying for ERC and ask for support letters from existing users through the GenABEL project infrastructure (forum news, GenABEL.org news)? Basically, by collecting these letters I will try to prove to the reviewers that GenABEL is already stable framework, it is something really used and useful, something worth investment in. 

