From rob.hooft at nbic.nl  Tue Oct 25 08:36:47 2011
From: rob.hooft at nbic.nl (Rob Hooft)
Date: Tue, 25 Oct 2011 08:36:47 +0200
Subject: [GenABEL-dev] GenABEL governance: inclusion of new packages
In-Reply-To: <1319487093.30301.2.camel@sidonia>
References: <1319127504.5948.269.camel@sidonia>
	<1319487093.30301.2.camel@sidonia>
Message-ID: <CAEco4NHsHeD2sXLZvWP6Of7jyFWxOQB7JXim2guEhEpoFS72iA@mail.gmail.com>

Lennart,

One important issue I am not hearing about in this discussion is "code
ownership". Who owns the rights to the code in the package? Are those
all the developers that ever contributed? Or their institutions? Is
any external code used that may have license restrictions? Or is
ownership "transferred" to a central authority in some way?

Only the owner of the code can decide about the licensing conditions
(this is not always the author!), so if ownership has not been
arranged, a change of licensing may be hard or nearly impossible.

NuGO is dealing with a very similar issue regarding their DBxP
software; contributing institutes (including NBIC) are now
brainstorming about different ways to set up a software foundation to
deal with this. The idea is that each of the contributors signs away
their ownership rights to the foundation, and the foundation (governed
by the contributors) can decide about licensing. It may be that the
outcome of this effort is a general "bioinformatics software
foundation" that can govern different software packages. Joining such
an effort may be an idea for GenABEL as well.

Something else I have not seen yet is a discussion about dual (or
multiple) licensing. The idea there is that some "customers" may be
willing to pay to lift restrictions of the GPL. If the ownership is
well arranged, such sales could be used to pay for future development
or for governance of the code.

Best regards,

Rob

